**demonstrates strong accuracy on trained KGs.** Table tab:webqsp_cwq_performance_efficiency_comparison_colored reports Hits@1 and token efficiency on and . Despite being trained from a lightweight Qwen2.5-3B base model, achieves the strongest overall performance among all compared methods on , reaching a Hits@1 of **82.75**, outperforming RoG built on Qwen2.5-7B (79.89), Qwen2.5-72B (81.63), and even Qwen3-235B (81.68). It also exceeds or is comparable to stronger multi-module baselines such as ToG (75.67), PoG (79.13), and ReKnoS (84.73) while using a substantially smaller backbone. On the more compositional , achieves a Hits@1 of **65.31**, improving over RoG by a large margin across all scales (46.32--48.94), and surpassing ReKnoS built on Qwen2.5-7B (53.45), Qwen2.5-72B (60.88), and Qwen3-235B (55.63). Notably, remains highly competitive with ReKnoS built on Qwen3-235B (65.31 vs. 65.63 on ), despite using a Qwen2.5-3B backbone that is nearly two orders of magnitude smaller. This highlights that reinforcement learning over structured KG actions can substantially improve reasoning effectiveness beyond what is achievable by model scaling alone.

**achieves efficient inference with reduced generation cost.** Table tab:webqsp_cwq_performance_efficiency_comparison_colored also reports total and generated tokens. On , uses only **308.67** generation tokens, which is 3.05$\times$ fewer than ToG (941.52) and 1.81$\times$ fewer than ReKnoS (592.38), while achieving higher Hits@1 than ToG and comparable performance to ReKnoS. On , generates **311.14** tokens, reducing generation cost by 2.58$\times$ compared to ToG (802.97) and 2.20$\times$ compared to ReKnoS (684.38), while achieving comparable answer accuracy to ReKnoS and outperforming ToG. Compared with the most token-efficient KG baseline RoG, uses moderately more generation tokens (308.67 vs. 254.31 on WebQSP; 311.14 vs. 252.45 on CWQ), but delivers substantially higher answer accuracy (+1.07 to +20.26 absolute Hits@1 depending on model scale and dataset). This indicates a favorable effectiveness-efficiency tradeoff: preserves competitive token efficiency while substantially improving reasoning quality.

**Single-agent design reduces system complexity.** Beyond token efficiency, simplifies the KG-RAG pipeline into a single-agent architecture. Prior methods typically require 2--5 modules (e.g., retrieval, planning, verification, and answering), whereas performs retrieval and reasoning within a unified policy using only one module. This reduces deployment complexity and avoids error propagation across stages. Despite this simplification, remains competitive or superior to multi-module systems across both datasets, showing that structured RL can effectively consolidate KG interaction and reasoning into a lightweight unified agent.

**Latency and throughput analysis.** On a single NVIDIA H100 GPU, we measure (i) single-query end-to-end latency and (ii) batched throughput. The single-query latency averages $6.4\pm1.5$ s per question, and the batched throughput reaches $3.7$ samples per second with a batch size of 64. The results suggest the feasibility of 's single-node deployment. See Appendix appendix:latency_memory_delopyment for the full results.

